select energy_manager.load_cards($cards$[
{"id":"p3-b02-032","paper":3,"topic":"Electric Motors","kind":"fact","front":"What usually happens to a motor's efficiency after rewinding?","back":"It usually falls. Careful rewinding can keep it at the earlier level, and with proper design measures it can sometimes be improved.","explain":"Over 50% of motors in some industries have been rewound. Stripping old windings with heat damages lamination insulation and raises eddy current losses, and a smaller wire diameter raises resistance and I²R losses.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 02 p83","order":3169},
{"id":"p3-b02-033","paper":3,"topic":"Electric Motors","kind":"fact","front":"What simple record shows whether a rewound motor was rewound well?","back":"A log of no-load input current for each motor. A rising no-load current indicates a poor-quality rewind.","explain":"Keep the no-load input power and speed from the time of purchase so the effect of rewinding can be checked. Reviewing the rewind shop's procedures also gives an indication of quality.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 02 p84","order":3170},
{"id":"p3-b02-034","paper":3,"topic":"Electric Motors","kind":"fact","front":"What is the difference between constant-torque and variable-torque loads, with examples?","back":"Constant torque: conveyors, rotary kilns and constant-displacement pumps, where torque stays the same across speed. Variable torque: centrifugal pumps and fans, where torque varies with the square of speed.","explain":"Power in variable torque loads varies with the cube of speed, so VSDs save the most energy there. Machine tools are a typical constant-power load, where torque falls as speed rises.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 02 p85","order":3171},
{"id":"p3-b02-035","paper":3,"topic":"Electric Motors","kind":"fact","front":"What speed options does a multi-speed motor offer?","back":"A single winding gives two speeds in a 2:1 ratio. Two separate windings give four speeds in total.","explain":"Multi-speed motors suit applications needing two or four fixed speeds rather than continuous speed control, and they are economical for that. They have lower efficiency than single-speed motors.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 02 p85","order":3172},
{"id":"p3-b02-036","paper":3,"topic":"Electric Motors","kind":"fact","front":"When is slip power recovery an economical speed control method?","back":"Only in relatively high-power applications where the motor speed range is 1:5 or less.","explain":"It is used with slip-ring motors. Instead of wasting slip power in resistors, it returns the excess power from the slip rings to the shaft or the supply line.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 02 p86","order":3173},
{"id":"p3-b02-037","paper":3,"topic":"Electric Motors","kind":"fact","front":"Why does a VFD change voltage together with frequency rather than frequency alone?","back":"To keep the magnetic flux nearly constant, so torque stays nearly constant up to base speed. Above base speed the voltage cannot rise, so the flux weakens and torque falls.","explain":"Lowering frequency alone would raise the flux and could saturate the core. This constant V/f control is the most popular VFD control method in industry.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 02 p87","order":3174},
{"id":"p3-b02-038","paper":3,"topic":"Electric Motors","kind":"fact","front":"How does a fluid coupling vary the speed of driven equipment without changing the motor speed?","back":"It works on the hydrodynamic principle: oil carries power from the impeller on the motor side to the runner on the load side. A speed difference (slip) is needed for the oil to flow.","explain":"Maximum torque occurs at standstill and sets the limiting torque, so the coupling also acts as a torque limiter. A fusible plug drains the oil on sustained overload.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 02 p90","order":3175},
{"id":"p3-b02-039","paper":3,"topic":"Electric Motors","kind":"fact","front":"Which efficiency classes are used in the star labelling scheme for energy-efficient induction motors?","back":"IE2, IE2(+), IE3, IE3(+) and IE3(++), based on IS 12615:2011. The scheme covers 2, 4 and 6 pole continuous duty motors rated 0.37 to 375 kW.","explain":"The schedule sets the rated output, efficiency class, label validity requirements, performance criteria, test report format and label design. It is run under the BEE S & L programme on energy-efficient motors.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 02 p91","order":3176},
{"id":"p3-b02-040","paper":3,"topic":"Electric Motors","kind":"problem","front":"A 37 kW, 415 V, 66 A, 0.88 PF motor runs at 410 V, 49 A and 0.76 PF for 7000 h/yr at Rs 6 per kWh. A 30 kW, 92% energy-efficient motor costs Rs 75,000 and the old motor's salvage value is Rs 10,000. Find the rated efficiency, present loading, annual saving and payback.","back":"Rated efficiency 88.63%; present loading 63.3%; annual saving about Rs 40,740; payback about 1.59 years.","explain":"This is the chapter's solved example for replacing an existing motor with an energy-efficient one. Only the efficiency difference matters, because the same shaft output is delivered.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 02 p92","steps":[{"prompt":"Find the rated input power.","answer":"1.732 × 0.415 × 66 × 0.88 = 41.75 kW.","explain":"Rated input comes from the nameplate voltage, current and PF, before any efficiency is applied."},{"prompt":"Find the rated efficiency.","answer":"37/41.746 = 88.63%.","explain":"Rated efficiency is rated output divided by rated input."},{"prompt":"Find the present input power, loading and shaft output.","answer":"Input = 1.732 × 0.410 × 49 × 0.76 = 26.44 kW. Loading = 26.44/41.746 = 63.3%. Shaft output = 37 × 0.633 = 23.44 kW.","explain":"Loading uses the measured kW input, not the ratio of currents."},{"prompt":"Find the loading of the new 30 kW motor.","answer":"23.44/30 = 78%.","explain":"78% loading is within the 75 to 100% band where energy-efficient motors keep their efficiency."},{"prompt":"Find the annual energy and cost saving.","answer":"Saving = 23.44 × (1/0.8863 − 1/0.92) × 7000 = 6790 kWh; × Rs 6 = Rs 40,740.","explain":"The saving comes from the difference in reciprocal efficiencies applied to the same shaft output."},{"prompt":"Find the payback period.","answer":"(75,000 − 10,000)/40,740 = 1.59 years.","explain":"The salvage value of the existing motor reduces the net investment."}],"order":3177},
{"id":"p3-b02-041","paper":3,"topic":"Electric Motors","kind":"problem","front":"A 7.5 kW, 415 V, 14.5 A, 1460 rpm induction motor with 88% full-load efficiency draws 10.1 A and 5.1 kW. What is its percentage loading?","back":"About 60% (option a).","explain":"Loading must be found from kW input against rated input, not from the current ratio. The current ratio gives about 70%, which is the trap answer.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 02 p93","steps":[{"prompt":"Find the rated input power.","answer":"7.5/0.88 = 8.52 kW.","explain":"Rated input = rated output ÷ rated efficiency."},{"prompt":"Take the measured input power.","answer":"5.1 kW (given).","explain":"Loading uses measured kW, not nameplate current."},{"prompt":"Calculate the percentage loading.","answer":"5.1/8.52 × 100 = 59.8%, about 60%.","explain":"Option a (60%) is the correct answer."},{"prompt":"Check the current-ratio trap.","answer":"10.1/14.5 = 70%, which is not the loading.","explain":"The chapter warns that loading should not be estimated as the ratio of currents, because power factor and efficiency change with load."}],"order":3178},
{"id":"p3-b02-042","paper":3,"topic":"Electric Motors","kind":"problem","front":"A 4-pole, 50 Hz induction motor has 1% slip. What is its actual speed?","back":"1485 rpm (option c).","explain":"Slip reduces speed below synchronous speed, and the rotor can never reach the stator field speed while it is driving a load.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 02 p93","steps":[{"prompt":"Find the synchronous speed.","answer":"120 × 50/4 = 1500 rpm.","explain":"Synchronous speed depends only on frequency and number of poles."},{"prompt":"Apply the slip.","answer":"Speed = 1500 × (1 − 0.01) = 1485 rpm.","explain":"Slip is the fraction by which the rotor lags the synchronous speed."},{"prompt":"Check the options.","answer":"1500 rpm would mean zero slip, which a loaded induction motor cannot have; 1515 rpm is above synchronous speed.","explain":"The rotor always runs slower than the stator field because it needs relative motion to induce current."}],"order":3179},
{"id":"p3-b02-043","paper":3,"topic":"Electric Motors","kind":"problem","front":"A 7.5 kW, 415 V, 14.5 A, 4-pole, 50 Hz motor has 89% full-load efficiency and 0.88 PF. An audit measures 410 V, 9.5 A, PF 0.8, 49.8 Hz and 1480 rpm. Find the input power, percentage loading and percentage slip.","back":"Input about 5.40 kW; loading about 64%; slip about 0.9%.","explain":"Measured values replace the nameplate PF and frequency. The measured frequency gives the correct synchronous speed for the slip calculation.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 02 p94","steps":[{"prompt":"Find the input power.","answer":"√3 × 0.410 × 9.5 × 0.8 = 5.40 kW.","explain":"Use the measured PF of 0.8, not the nameplate 0.88."},{"prompt":"Find the rated input power.","answer":"7.5/0.89 = 8.43 kW.","explain":"Rated input = rated output ÷ rated efficiency."},{"prompt":"Find the percentage loading.","answer":"5.40/8.43 × 100 = 64%.","explain":"Loading is based on kW input rather than on current."},{"prompt":"Find the synchronous speed at the measured frequency.","answer":"120 × 49.8/4 = 1494 rpm.","explain":"The supply frequency is slightly below 50 Hz, so the synchronous speed is slightly lower too."},{"prompt":"Find the percentage slip.","answer":"(1494 − 1480)/1494 × 100 = 0.94%, about 0.9%.","explain":"Using 1500 rpm would give 1.3%, but the measured frequency gives the correct synchronous speed."}],"order":3180},
{"id":"p3-b02-044","paper":3,"topic":"Electric Motors","kind":"problem","front":"A 50 HP, 415 V, 60 A, 1475 rpm, delta-connected standard motor shows 415 V, 18 A and 1080 W at no load (50 Hz), with 0.27 Ω stator resistance per phase. Estimate the iron plus friction and windage losses, full-load stator copper loss at 120 °C, slip, motor input, and full-load efficiency and PF. Take stray loss as 0.5% of rated output.","back":"Iron + F&W about 992.5 W; R₁₂₀ about 0.355 Ω; stator I²R about 1278 W; slip 1.67%; rotor input about 37.9 kW; motor input about 40.4 kW; efficiency about 92.3%; PF about 0.94.","explain":"This follows the same method as the chapter's worked example. The 0.27 Ω reading is assumed to be taken at the 35 °C ambient, which should be checked against the test record.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 02 p94","steps":[{"prompt":"Find the stator I²R loss at no load and the iron plus friction and windage losses.","answer":"3 × (18/√3)² × 0.27 = 87.5 W. Iron + F&W = 1080 − 87.5 = 992.5 W.","explain":"No-load input minus stator copper loss leaves the core and friction and windage losses."},{"prompt":"Correct the stator resistance to 120 °C.","answer":"0.27 × (120 + 235)/(35 + 235) = 0.355 Ω per phase.","explain":"The 35 °C ambient is assumed as the reference temperature of the 0.27 Ω reading."},{"prompt":"Find the full-load stator copper loss at 120 °C.","answer":"3 × (60/√3)² × 0.355 = 1278 W.","explain":"Hot resistance and full-load current give the operating-temperature copper loss."},{"prompt":"Find the slip and the rotor input.","answer":"Slip = (1500 − 1475)/1500 = 0.0167. Output = 50 HP × 746 W = 37300 W. Rotor input = 37300/(1 − 0.0167) = 37933 W.","explain":"1 HP = 746 W, and rotor input = output ÷ (1 − slip)."},{"prompt":"Find the motor input.","answer":"37933 + 1278 + 992.5 + 186.5 (0.5% of 37300) = 40390 W ≈ 40.39 kW.","explain":"Stray loss is taken at the standard 0.5% of rated output."},{"prompt":"Find the full-load efficiency and power factor.","answer":"Efficiency = 37300/40390 = 92.3%. PF = 40390/(√3 × 415 × 60) ≈ 0.94.","explain":"Efficiency is output over input, and PF is input kW over the apparent power at full-load current."}],"order":3181},
{"id":"p3-b03-001","paper":3,"topic":"Compressed Air System","kind":"fact","front":"What share of the prime mover's energy reaches the point of end-use in a compressed air system?","back":"Only about 10–30%. The remaining 70–90% is lost as unusable heat, with smaller losses to friction, misuse and noise.","explain":"Figure 3.1 is a Sankey diagram of this energy flow. The book's message is that simple, cost-effective measures on the demand side can save around 30%.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 03 p97","order":3182},
{"id":"p3-b03-002","paper":3,"topic":"Compressed Air System","kind":"fact","front":"Name the two broad classes of air compressor.","back":"Positive displacement compressors (pressure raised by reducing volume) and dynamic compressors (air velocity converted into pressure).","explain":"Positive displacement machines are reciprocating or rotary. Dynamic machines are centrifugal, either radial or axial flow. Flow and pressure requirements decide which type suits a given application.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 03 p97","order":3183},
{"id":"p3-b03-003","paper":3,"topic":"Compressed Air System","kind":"fact","front":"What are the four most widely used reciprocating compressor configurations?","back":"Horizontal, vertical, horizontal balance-opposed and tandem.","explain":"Vertical units cover about 50–150 cfm. Horizontal balance-opposed units cover 200–5000 cfm in multi-stage designs and up to 10,000 cfm in single-stage designs.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 03 p99","order":3184},
{"id":"p3-b03-004","paper":3,"topic":"Compressed Air System","kind":"fact","front":"What discharge temperatures do two-stage and single-stage reciprocating compressors reach?","back":"Two-stage: about 140–160°C. Single-stage: about 205–240°C.","explain":"Two-stage machines are used for high pressures. Their lower discharge temperature is one reason multi-stage units can have lower specific power than single-stage units over the same pressure range.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 03 p99","order":3185},
{"id":"p3-b03-005","paper":3,"topic":"Compressed Air System","kind":"fact","front":"What is the most common rotary air compressor, and how does it compress air?","back":"The single-stage helical or spiral oil-flooded screw compressor. Two rotors compress air inside a casing with no valves. Oil seals the clearances and cools the air, and must be separated from the discharge air.","explain":"Cooling takes place inside the compressor, so the working parts never see extreme temperatures. Oil-free screw types also exist, using specially designed air ends.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 03 p99","order":3186},
{"id":"p3-b03-006","paper":3,"topic":"Compressed Air System","kind":"fact","front":"Why is the reported isothermal efficiency of a compressor usually lower than its adiabatic efficiency?","back":"Isothermal power excludes the power needed to overcome friction, so isothermal efficiency comes out lower. Manufacturers usually report the isothermal figure, so check the basis before comparing machines.","explain":"Both figures measure overall efficiency, covering the compressor and its drive motor. The book flags this as an important consideration when selecting compressors from reported efficiency values.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 03 p102","order":3187},
{"id":"p3-b03-007","paper":3,"topic":"Compressed Air System","kind":"problem","front":"Isothermal power is P₁ × Q × ln(r) / 36.7 kW, with P₁ and P₂ in kg/cm² absolute, Q free air in m³/h and r = P₂/P₁. Find the isothermal power for a 1000 m³/h FAD compressing from 1.0 to 8.0 kg/cm² abs, and its isothermal efficiency if the motor input is 70 kW.","back":"About 56.7 kW of isothermal power, giving an isothermal efficiency of about 81%.","explain":"The 36.7 divisor absorbs the unit conversion for kg/cm² and m³/h. Isothermal power is the ideal minimum work for the duty, so dividing by measured input gives the overall efficiency of compressor and motor.","flash":false,"steps":[{"prompt":"Find the pressure ratio r.","answer":"r = P₂ / P₁ = 8.0 / 1.0 = 8.","explain":"Both pressures must be absolute for the book's formula."},{"prompt":"Find ln(r).","answer":"ln 8 = 2.079.","explain":"The formula uses the natural logarithm (the OCR shows it as log_e r)."},{"prompt":"Compute the isothermal power.","answer":"P_iso = 1.0 × 1000 × 2.079 / 36.7 = 56.7 kW.","explain":"Q is free air delivered in m³/h, not m³/min."},{"prompt":"Compute the isothermal efficiency with a 70 kW motor input.","answer":"η_iso = 56.7 / 70 = 0.81, about 81%.","explain":"Isothermal efficiency is isothermal power divided by actual measured input power."}],"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 03 p102","order":3188},
{"id":"p3-b03-008","paper":3,"topic":"Compressed Air System","kind":"fact","front":"Name the major components of a compressed air system.","back":"Intake air filters, inter-stage coolers, after-coolers, air dryers, moisture drain traps, receivers, piping network, filters, regulators and lubricators.","explain":"Filters keep dust out of the compressor, coolers remove heat and moisture, dryers supply dry air for instruments, and receivers damp pulsation and store air. Regulators and lubricators sit as close as possible to the equipment they serve.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 03 p103","order":3189},
{"id":"p3-b03-009","paper":3,"topic":"Compressed Air System","kind":"fact","front":"What is the thumb rule for compressor inlet air temperature?","back":"Every 4°C drop in inlet air temperature lowers energy consumption by about 1% for the same output.","explain":"The compressor room runs warmer than ambient because of compression heat, so draw cool air from outside. Use a large-diameter suction duct with few bends to limit pressure drop on the intake.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 03 p104","order":3190},
{"id":"p3-b03-010","paper":3,"topic":"Compressed Air System","kind":"fact","front":"How much does a 250 mm WC pressure drop across a choked intake filter raise compressor power?","back":"About 2% for the same output. Table 3.4 gives 1.6% at 200 mm WC, 3.2% at 400, 4.7% at 600 and 7.0% at 800 mm WC.","explain":"Fit manometers or differential pressure gauges across the filter to plan cleaning and replacement. Choose the filter for the compressor type and install it as close to the compressor as possible.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 03 p104","order":3191},
{"id":"p3-b03-011","paper":3,"topic":"Compressed Air System","kind":"fact","front":"How much water vapour does 1000 m³/min of air at 30°C carry at 100% relative humidity, according to Table 3.5?","back":"About 68.22 kg per hour. The table gives 45.00 kg/h at 80% RH and 27.60 kg/h at 50% RH.","explain":"Moisture carrying capacity rises with temperature and falls with pressure. Intake near a cooling tower or a dryer exhaust adds moisture, so the book says to avoid it.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 03 p105","order":3192},
{"id":"p3-b03-012","paper":3,"topic":"Compressed Air System","kind":"problem","front":"Table 3.8 gives a minimum cooling water flow of 18.9 L/min for a two-stage compressor with after-cooler at 2.85 m³/min FAD and 7 bar. Estimate the minimum for a 5.7 m³/min machine of the same type, assuming flow scales linearly with FAD.","back":"About 37.8 L/min. This is an estimate; the book gives only the 2.85 m³/min figure.","explain":"Linear scaling is an assumption made for this exercise, not a rule stated in the book. Confirm cooling water needs with the compressor maker's data, since both too little flow and too cold water cause problems.","flash":false,"steps":[{"prompt":"Find the capacity ratio.","answer":"5.7 / 2.85 = 2.","explain":"The reference machine in Table 3.8 is 2.85 m³/min."},{"prompt":"Scale the minimum cooling water flow.","answer":"18.9 × 2 = 37.8 L/min.","explain":"Assumes linear scaling with FAD at the same 7 bar."},{"prompt":"State the check before sizing the pump.","answer":"Verify against the compressor maker's cooling water data.","explain":"The book warns that very low cooling water temperature can condense moisture in the air and damage cylinders."}],"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 03 p107","order":3193},
{"id":"p3-b03-013","paper":3,"topic":"Compressed Air System","kind":"fact","front":"A compressor is set at 6–7 kg/cm². At what pressures does it load and unload?","back":"It loads (cuts in) at 6 kg/cm² and unloads (cuts out) at 7 kg/cm², with a pressure switch doing the switching.","explain":"The book says cut-in and cut-out should be set at optimum levels, and the gap between them kept as narrow as practical.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 03 p107","order":3194},
{"id":"p3-b03-014","paper":3,"topic":"Compressed Air System","kind":"fact","front":"How much input power does a 1 bar reduction in delivery pressure save?","back":"About 6–10%. The book's example: cutting delivery from 8 to 7 kg/cm² saves about 9% of input power.","explain":"Before lowering the setting, study the pressure needs of each equipment and the pressure drop between generation and use. Lower delivery pressure also gives better volumetric efficiency.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 03 p108","order":3195},
{"id":"p3-b03-015","paper":3,"topic":"Compressed Air System","kind":"fact","front":"Several compressors feed one header. Which one should be the modulating compressor?","back":"If sizes differ, only the smallest compressor modulates. If types differ, the one with the lowest no-load or part-load power modulates, and the others run near full load.","explain":"Grade compressors by specific energy consumption at each pressure, and let the most efficient ones carry most of the demand.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 03 p108","order":3196},
{"id":"p3-b03-016","paper":3,"topic":"Compressed Air System","kind":"problem","front":"Five reciprocating compressors (A to E) serve one plant section on a 45-minute cycle. A to D run full time and E loads for 5.88 min. Capacities at 7 kg/cm² are 13.17, 12.32, 13.14, 12.75 and 13.65 m³/min. Find the air generated on line, the consumption rate and the air drawl as a percentage of capacity on line.","back":"Capacity on line is 2926.35 m³ per cycle. Air consumed is 2392.36 m³ per cycle, or 53.16 m³/min. Air drawl is 81.75%.","explain":"The section draws only about four-fifths of what the compressors could make. The book suggests keeping one compressor in cycling mode and replacing the one with the highest unload losses with a smaller unit.","flash":false,"steps":[{"prompt":"Find the capacity on line for the 45-minute cycle.","answer":"45 × (13.17 + 12.32 + 13.14 + 12.75 + 13.65) = 45 × 65.03 = 2926.35 m³.","explain":"Each machine's capacity is multiplied by the full cycle time, as the book does."},{"prompt":"Find the air actually drawn (consumed) per cycle.","answer":"45×13.17 + 45×12.32 + 45×13.14 + 45×12.75 + 5.88×13.65 = 2392.36 m³.","explain":"Compressor E only contributes for its 5.88 min of loading, not the full cycle."},{"prompt":"Find the consumption rate.","answer":"2392.36 / 45 = 53.16 m³/min.","explain":"Cycle output divided by cycle length gives the average rate the section draws."},{"prompt":"Find the air drawl as a percentage of capacity on line.","answer":"2392.36 / 2926.35 × 100 = 81.75%.","explain":"The remaining 18.25% is capacity the section does not use."}],"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 03 p108","order":3197},
{"id":"p3-b03-017","paper":3,"topic":"Compressed Air System","kind":"fact","front":"What pressure drop is acceptable in the mains header at the farthest point, and in the distribution system?","back":"0.3 bar in the mains header at the farthest point, and 0.5 bar in the distribution system.","explain":"Excess drop from small pipe, choked filters or undersized couplings forces a higher generation pressure and wastes power. The design limit from compressor to the most distant point of use is 5%.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 03 p110","order":3198},
{"id":"p3-b03-018","paper":3,"topic":"Compressed Air System","kind":"problem","front":"Table 3.11 gives a power loss of 9.5 kW for 170 m³/h (100 cfm) through a 40 mm bore pipe, and 1.2 kW through a 65 mm bore pipe. At 8000 h/yr and Rs 5/kWh (the book's basis), what is the annual saving from upsizing 40 mm to 65 mm?","back":"About 8.3 kW, or 66,400 kWh and about Rs 3.3 lakh per year, before the cost of the larger pipe.","explain":"The book says present energy prices justify liberal pipe sizing to reduce pressure drop. An undersized bore wastes energy every hour it runs, so the saving recurs each year.","flash":false,"steps":[{"prompt":"Find the power saved per hour.","answer":"9.5 − 1.2 = 8.3 kW.","explain":"Table 3.11 gives the equivalent power loss for the stated flow in each pipe size."},{"prompt":"Find the annual energy saved.","answer":"8.3 × 8000 = 66,400 kWh/yr.","explain":"Uses the 8000 operating hours assumed in the book's leakage cost table."},{"prompt":"Find the annual money saved.","answer":"66,400 × Rs 5 = Rs 3,32,000, about Rs 3.3 lakh/yr.","explain":"Rs 5/kWh is the book's tariff assumption. The extra pipe cost must be netted off to find the payback."}],"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 03 p110","order":3199},
{"id":"p3-b03-019","paper":3,"topic":"Compressed Air System","kind":"fact","front":"What are the main capacity control methods for compressors, and what do they cost in power?","back":"On/off control (best for small units, as motor idle losses are eliminated); load/unload (unloaded power up to 30% of full load); multi-step (0, 25, 50, 75 and 100% steps); throttling or speed control for centrifugal units.","explain":"Table 3.13 shows a typical reciprocating machine at about 80% of full-load power at 75% load, 60% at 50% load and 40% at 25% load.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 03 p111","order":3200},
{"id":"p3-b03-020","paper":3,"topic":"Compressed Air System","kind":"fact","front":"Above what share of system volume does speed control beat vane control for a centrifugal compressor?","back":"Above about 40% of system volume, speed control is recommended. Below 40%, vane control can draw less power.","explain":"Below 40% the speed control system itself becomes inefficient, which is why vane control can win at very low flow. Vane control works through adjustable inlet guide vanes.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 03 p112","order":3201},
{"id":"p3-b03-021","paper":3,"topic":"Compressed Air System","kind":"problem","front":"A paper plant conveyed wood chips with compressed air at an equivalent 77 kW. A blower now does the job at 7 kW. What is the saving in kW and in percent, and the annual energy saved at 8000 h/yr?","back":"70 kW saved, about 91% of the original power. That is 560,000 kWh/yr at 8000 h/yr.","explain":"The book uses this case to show that blower conveying is far cheaper than compressed air for low-pressure material handling.","flash":false,"steps":[{"prompt":"Find the power saved.","answer":"77 − 7 = 70 kW.","explain":"Same conveying duty, different air supply."},{"prompt":"Find the percentage saved.","answer":"70 / 77 × 100 = 90.9%, about 91%.","explain":"Compares the saving with the original compressed air load."},{"prompt":"Find the annual energy saved.","answer":"70 × 8000 = 560,000 kWh/yr.","explain":"Uses the 8000 operating hours that the book assumes in its leakage cost example."}],"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 03 p112","order":3202},
{"id":"p3-b03-022","paper":3,"topic":"Compressed Air System","kind":"fact","front":"What is the book's guidance on using compressed air for cleaning or blowing dust?","back":"Use blowers or local fans for moving air where possible. If compressed air is unavoidable, use blow guns and keep pressure below 2 bar. A 1/4 inch hose left open at 7 bar for 1000 h/yr costs about Rs 1 lakh per year.","explain":"Misuse such as body cleaning, floor cleaning and liquid agitation should be discouraged. Where cleaning air is essential, use a separate cleaning header with a main isolation valve opened only for defined periods.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 03 p113","order":3203},
{"id":"p3-b03-023","paper":3,"topic":"Compressed Air System","kind":"fact","front":"What does a 3.1 mm orifice leak waste, and what does it cost per year, according to Table 3.17?","back":"About 3.0 kW, costing about Rs 1.2 lakh per year (Rs 120,000) at Rs 5/kWh, 8000 h/yr and 7 bar.","explain":"Even small leaks are costly: a 0.8 mm orifice wastes 0.2 kW (Rs 8000/yr). The book says the cost of stopping leaks is repaid through energy savings.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 03 p114","order":3204}
]$cards$::jsonb);
